<interrogationes unitas="ludus_fenestra">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I render frames or take a screenshot without opening a window?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_pixelorum_creare_nuda ludus_fenestra_creare ludus_quadrum ludus_fenestra_imaginem_scribere" via="probationes/probatio_ludus_imago.c">Make
      a bare table with tabula_pixelorum_creare_nuda, pass it to
      ludus_fenestra_creare, call ludus_quadrum(lf, nunc) once per
      frame, then ludus_fenestra_imaginem_scribere(lf, via) to write
      the last frame as a PNG. That function returns FALSUM before
      the first frame and when the write fails. The worklog records a
      real-window PNG that was byte-identical to the headless one, so
      headless frames are faithful screenshots. The apps expose this
      as -imago with -fumus.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep lf->mandata or anything from the previous frame across ludus_quadrum calls?
    <responsum symbolum="ludus_quadrum ludus_fenestra_imaginem_scribere piscina_vacare">No.
      Each ludus_quadrum pulses the dispatcher, then calls
      piscina_vacare on the per-frame piscina and builds a fresh
      Mandata there, so lf-&gt;mandata and
      everything allocated for the last frame (including the PNG
      buffers of ludus_fenestra_imaginem_scribere) is valid only
      until the next frame. Copy out anything you need first.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the window loop sleep when idle, and does that delay input?
    <responsum symbolum="ludus_fenestra_mora fenestra_expectare_eventus dispensator_pulsare" via="lib/ludus_fenestra.worklog.md">Until
      2026-10-05 it polled without waiting, and an idle pictor window
      pinned a CPU core. Now it blocks in fenestra_expectare_eventus
      for ludus_fenestra_mora(lf): ZEPHYRUM before the first frame,
      then the dispatcher's quies_ms. An arriving event wakes the
      wait at once, so drags and strokes are not delayed. The quiet
      flush still runs in each frame's dispensator_pulsare.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I keep a live app (a terminal, a clock) updating when no events arrive?
    <responsum symbolum="ludus_fenestra_pulsum_ponere ludus_fenestra_pingendum LudusPulsator" via="apps/vicus/vicus.c">Install
      a pulse with ludus_fenestra_pulsum_ponere(lf, fn, ctx); NIHIL
      removes it. In the normal loop (quadra_maxima ZEPHYRUM) fn(ctx)
      runs once per iteration, inside ludus_fenestra_pingendum, and
      with a pulse every wait is XVI ms at most (also the first one,
      which would otherwise be ZEPHYRUM). The loop draws only when
      ludus_fenestra_pingendum says so: there were events, the pulse
      returned VERUM, no frame has been drawn yet, or (after the
      dispatcher alone is pulsed for its timers) the store version
      differs from the one at the last drawn frame. So return VERUM
      from the pulse whenever it changed something visible. Without a
      pulse every iteration draws, as before.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the frame skip compare the store version instead of counting recompositions?
    <responsum symbolum="ludus_fenestra_pingendum insula_versio ludus_quadrum" via="lib/ludus_fenestra.worklog.md">The
      dispatcher recomposes on EVERY event, including the empty
      pulse (the staleness rule), so recompositions do not mean
      anything changed. insula_versio goes up once per accepted write.
      It is compared with versio_picta, which ludus_quadrum records,
      rather than with the value before this pulse, because a click
      handled in an earlier iteration may still be undrawn.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use quadra_maxima for a smoke run of an idle app that installs a pulse?
    <responsum symbolum="ludus_fenestra_pingendum ludus_fenestra_mora">Yes.
      Under -fumus the shell's loop (lib/testa.c, once
      ludus_fenestra_currere) passes quadra_maxima XXX: the frame skip
      is bypassed and every iteration draws, so the loop stops after
      exactly that many frames. Before the 2026-10-08 fix an idle vicus -fumus drew
      one frame and hung. Two consequences: in smoke mode
      ludus_fenestra_pingendum is never called, so your pulse function
      never runs (vicus -fumus does not poll its reactor); and with a
      pulse each idle wait is at most XVI ms, while an app without one
      waits the dispatcher's quies (300 ms in pictor) per idle frame,
      which made pictor's 30-frame smoke run take about 9 s.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what coordinates does the dispatcher see for window resizes and the first size?
    <responsum symbolum="ludus_fenestra_tractare tabula_pixelorum_ad_fenestram">Table
      pixels, never window points. On EVENTUS_MUTARE_MAGNITUDINEM,
      ludus_fenestra_tractare refits the table with
      tabula_pixelorum_ad_fenestram (scale kept, buffer grown in the
      piscina only when it gets bigger) and rewrites the event's size
      to the table's before passing it on. The very first event or
      frame first sends one synthetic MUTARE_MAGNITUDINEM with the
      table's size. An event with tempus ZEPHYRUM is stamped with
      nunc.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I destroy a LudusFenestra and free its frame memory?
    <responsum symbolum="ludus_fenestra_creare piscina_generare_dynamicum piscina_destruere">Not
      through the API: there is no destroy function. The struct lives
      in your piscina, but ludus_fenestra_creare also makes its own
      dynamic piscina for frames ("ludus_quadrum", LXIV * M initial),
      and nothing ever destroys it. Destroying your piscina does not
      release it. The field is public, so code that creates many (such
      as tests) can call piscina_destruere(lf-&gt;piscina_quadri) itself
      once it is done with lf; nothing in the tree does today. Apps
      create one per process.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
